359557} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6a5eac-a965-59f1-a085-89c92b83abbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb6a5eac-a965-59f1-a085-89c92b83abbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359558} :answer-field/id #uuid "bee8795e-5501-5d7c-8069-49b1ebd243b5" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9c0da7f-f06a-5e11-82dc-9399c7844b4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-342949" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x+1, & x\\in [-3,0] \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\in (0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ because $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/7736417b068541a1609efa46e9824cd3f65cd34856c1d2f5d0ec7258ec883b5c.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ because ${F}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(2x+1)=2(-3)+1=-5\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and II are false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069003/assets/ac4d89fcccca99bebaeab4b44baac9c5c03651c38cef7e4d14cfe57978d066b7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2e37d37-007c-569b-a44c-cb6a9d075371" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa2f53a3-53c4-558f-a41f-c4cea85856e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359561} {:answer/id #uuid "080d32b7-3cd8-5464-ab69-385abec8f215" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359562} {:answer/id #uuid "24c948de-ad54-5f58-b721-cedaf8598d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359563} {:answer/id #uuid "14ff844f-c6f6-5662-9a49-6c10eadc4e93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359564} {:answer/id #uuid "b9e6fe77-dc63-5fbc-9410-f7c6540e5577" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359565} {:answer/id #uuid "6706e0f3-929c-5ae6-b0b8-099d3d716b36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6706e0f3-929c-5ae6-b0b8-099d3d716b36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.