\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359625} {:answer/id #uuid "bc61008f-80f5-501a-b6ee-f64e941d77d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359626} {:answer/id #uuid "90681fa5-7d9a-59e9-b3f4-3f7958d8a7f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc61008f-80f5-501a-b6ee-f64e941d77d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359626} :answer-field/id #uuid "91140984-2cd4-551a-beb9-61580d98c75a" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4467f0cd-7b61-5702-b8db-33e570ded622" :question/math-academy-id "q-343339" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $2$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-4x$ defined on $[-1,1).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $2$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=-2{x}^{2}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-4x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-2{x}^{2}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=1$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-1,1).$ \nTherefore, the $2$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-4t)\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ [-2{t}^{2}]_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot [-\\frac{1}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi t)]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -2{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{8(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e01d04-6c13-52eb-a4b6-ad9a5cde8134" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7108327-ce2f-56d8-a2b7-fc6c4943ed62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359630} {:answer/id #uuid "28e0cae2-bed9-59d6-ab30-4f56e031b5bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359631} {:answer/id #uuid "2e055695-ba7b-5f6e-9466-c799b360239f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359632} {:answer/id #uuid "c22fb743-3cb8-5462-9845-912b168418c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e055695-ba7b-5f6e-9466-c799b360239f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359632} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f8ff5d7-4d52-5de4-aa