type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359640} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5e5573a-bb6d-5f3b-b897-11387fb8d543" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359639} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5b13052-8f59-59bf-bff8-f90af3e45850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359645} {:answer/id #uuid "7658b26b-389e-543f-bc94-83802d0997d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359646} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea052a2-66d0-5437-a11f-38005337b62b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359647} {:answer/id #uuid "492859fa-83e2-5911-93fc-ae37416b1f54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ea052a2-66d0-5437-a11f-38005337b62b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359647} :answer-field/id #uuid "83eed1f4-ad05-5bd6-b2b3-f4e99be097c1" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8437e095-a47c-5c6c-853f-484ef102aeab" :question/math-academy-id "q-343340" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $8$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=\\frac{3}{2}x$ defined on $[-4,4).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $8$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-4,4).$ \nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{3}{2}t\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ {[\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot [-\\frac{4}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}))\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e01d04-6c13-52eb-a4b6-ad9a5cde8134" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0056d1a6-09ee-5fab-8fdd-8b80847047e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{10})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359651} {:answer/id #u